{
  "requirement": "An execution error returns `Err` rather than a `Fingerprinted` containing a\ncompleted answer.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The obligation is that any execution error makes `verify` return `Err` instead of a `Fingerprinted` holding a completed answer. A negative answer is a completed answer and is not an error.\n\nThe marked tests cover the execution-error classes:\n- `validation_tests.rs#fn[runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls]` asserts that `verify` returns `Err(ValidationError::NoAgentConfigured)` when the criterion asks without an agent. It also asserts that no record is stored.\n- `validation_tests.rs#fn[resolution_storage_and_callback_errors_are_not_judgments]` asserts `Err(Graph(\"missing\"))` when a read inside the callback fails, with no records stored. It also covers the `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` failures.\n- `validation_tests.rs#fn[criterion_decode_errors_are_execution_errors_and_false_is_a_completed_answer]` asserts that a decode failure inside `execute` gives `Err(ValidationError::Output)` and stores nothing. It also asserts that a `false` answer is returned as an `Ok` `Fingerprinted` with its dependencies. That is the counter-case the requirement distinguishes.\n- `contracts.rs#fn[loading_execution_resolution_and_saving_failures_do_not_publish_answers]` covers an `execute` failure, a missing read, and a failing edge lookup. Each gives `Err(Graph(..))`, and nothing is written.\n- `contracts.rs#fn[typed_loading_reports_decode_errors_even_for_stale_evidence]`, `contracts.rs#fn[serialization_failure_leaves_the_existing_record_unchanged]` and `storage.rs#fn[malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors]` add output and decode errors, and storage errors that leave the existing record unchanged.\n\nThe agent-failure variant is checked through `verify_with_agent` in `validation_tests.rs#fn[invalid_typed_submission_is_an_agent_error]`. That test is not marked for this requirement. The path is the same error propagation as the other variants, so I don't count it as an adequacy gap.",
  "references": "e442e1f53b7c2fb2ca33ead5a99cf1be842ca8716fdc5e6e2a5537ed3459eaf8",
